course: dictionary
lesson: 5676
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A loaf is a big piece of bread, baked whole before it is cut into slices. «A loaf of bread» means one whole bread, and «a sliced loaf» is one that has already been cut up. The plural is loaves, so two loaves, not two loafs.
explain: The same word can mean different things. «Meat loaf» is minced meat cooked in a loaf shape, so the food and the shape tell you what it is. «To loaf around» means to spend time doing nothing, as in «He loafs around all Saturday.» Read the words around the word to find which meaning fits.
explain: Use the context to choose. In a maths problem, «A loaf has a set number of slices» tells you a loaf is a counted thing, so you can multiply loaves by the slices in each. In a story, «The Little Red Hen baked a loaf of bread» means she made bread. Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: A loaf is bread baked in one big piece, before it is cut into slices.
fact: The plural of loaf is loaves: one loaf, two loaves.
fact: «Half a loaf is better than none» means getting part of what you want is still good.
fact: «Use your loaf» is UK slang that means use your brain and think.
fact: Many people work to bring a «loaf of bread» to you: a farmer grows wheat, a miller grinds flour, a baker bakes it, a driver delivers it and a shopkeeper sells it.
exercise: What is the plural of loaf? | loafes | loavs | *loaves | loaffs
exercise: «For example, a girl throws the whole loaf away.» What is a loaf here? | A meat dish cooked in a tin | *A whole bread, baked in one piece | One slice of bread | Doing nothing at all
exercise: «To loaf around» means what? | To bake bread at the bakery | *To spend time doing nothing | To cook minced meat | To cut bread into slices
test: «A loaf has a set number of slices, and a shop sells only whole loaves.» What does a shop sell? | *A whole loaf | A slice | Crumbs | Half a slice
test: A baker makes 4 loaves, and each loaf has 12 slices. How many slices in all? | 16 | 52 | 36 | *48
exam: Mum says, «Use your loaf!» What does she mean? | Lie down and do nothing | *Think carefully about it | Bake a loaf of bread for tea | Cut the bread into slices
